#!/usr/bin/env -S python3 -I -B -S
"""Download one file over https and keep it only if its SHA-256 equals the pinned value (plan step P0.11.5).

Usage: fetch-pinned <https-url> <sha256> <destination>

Builds bring files in from the network through this program and nothing else; the pin gate refuses any other
download in a file a build executes. The program is used only as a command: each run is its own process, and the
functions below exist for its tests, not as a library promise.

Two processes. However it is started, the program first replaces itself with Python in isolated mode, without
bytecode or site hooks and with the variables named under Threat model removed, running this same file, so no module
beside it and no PYTHONPATH entry can take the place of a standard module; then, before it opens anything, it blocks
every signal except the few that report a fault in its own code (and SIGKILL and SIGSTOP, which nothing can block),
so no handler ever runs in it again and no step can be cut off half way. Its own process does every step on disk.
The network is reached by a separate process, started in its own process group with the same isolation and with the
URL and byte cap as arguments; it compiles this file from its source text, opens the URL, follows redirects only to
https, stops at the cap, and sends every byte it receives into a scratch file that this program made and noted
first, handed to it as its standard output. The command line has no option that runs that part alone, so the tool
offers no way to fetch without the check. Once that process has ended, its process group is killed and the program
copies the bytes into a second scratch file that nothing else ever had open, measuring and hashing the copy itself;
that copy is what gets placed. A request to stop (any pending signal whose default would end the process) is looked
for while the download runs, between chunks of a copy, as the download process ends and once more just before the
commit point. Found before that point, it ends the download and refuses the run, after the whole cleanup; found
later, it changes nothing. One deadline of 15 minutes bounds the request, any redirects and the body: when it
passes, the download process is killed, which also covers looking up the host. Its error output is read while it
runs, a bounded amount and never to its end, so nothing it or a server sends can stall the run.

How a run goes. The body lands in a hidden scratch file next to the destination and is renamed over the destination
only once its hash checks out. Whatever already sits at the destination is dealt with before anything else is
written. That first change to the destination moves whatever sits at that name to a scratch name, or deletes it when
the move is impossible or the run fails before reaching it. That old file is reused, with no download, only if a
fresh copy of it hashes to the pin; otherwise it and its copy are gone before the download starts. Until that first
change the program only reads, so a run killed at that stage finds the destination untouched; one that refuses
there removes the file, as every refusal removes a file it never checked.
Afterward the destination name is, at every moment, either vacant or holding a checked file, even if the process is
killed. The price is a short gap in which a good existing file is away from its name while it is checked.

Outcome and exit status. A run turns final at its commit point: the checked file is in place and one store marks the
run as placed. Before that store the cleanup undoes the placement; after it, nothing can. Each cleanup step runs even
when an earlier one failed. Hence a status of 1 always means no file was written, and a status of 0 always means one
was. A failure after a placement keeps status 0 and is printed as one warning line. The program flushes its own
output and leaves directly, so an output stream that is closed or out of space leaves the status alone.

Output. Every line printed is printable ASCII. Any other character, whether from a file name, a system error message,
the download process or anything a server sent back, appears as an escape, so no input can split or fake a log line.
Every exception ends as a single such line; the last-resort report prints one line and no chained text.

Permissions. Only the mode bits carry over: a checked copy of an existing file keeps its mode, but not its ACLs, flags
or extended attributes; a caller that needs those applies them after placement.

Threat model. Covered: an untrusted network; a destination holding an old, truncated or wrong file; files in the
repository beside the tool; and the hooks through which Python and OpenSSL take code from elsewhere (bytecode files,
site-packages .pth files, sitecustomize, PYTHONPATH and the other PYTHON variables, OPENSSL_CONF and the like, the
DYLD and LD variables, and the two that pick the interpreter on macOS, which make the start malformed). Not covered:
whatever launches the program is trusted, because it picks the interpreter, the packages installed with it and the
rest of the environment, and it can run code before this file is read; another process writing into the destination
folder during the run, since such a process already has the build's trust; a process the download process starts
that leaves its process group, which can only touch the first scratch file, never the copy that is placed; and
SIGKILL, SIGSTOP or a fault signal (SIGSEGV, SIGBUS and the like, left deliverable so that a genuine fault still
ends the program) sent to this process, or a signal in the instant between the interpreter starting and the block,
before anything is opened; after any of these the destination is still vacant or checked, and the download process
ends itself once this one is gone. To get the covered behavior from the first line, start it by its path, so its own
first line chooses the flags. The cleanup deletes a scratch name, or a file this run put at the destination, only
while that name still holds that exact file; any name left behind is listed, in the refusal or, after a placement,
in a warning.

Inputs. The URL must be https, name a host, and carry no user name or password. The digest is 64 hex digits. The
destination must lie inside the working folder (an absolute path may reach it through the shell's $PWD spelling when
that resolves to the same place); every folder on its path must exist and be a real folder, not a link, checked one
level at a time and kept open for the write; the destination itself is either missing or a regular file; it is valid
UTF-8 without control characters, it plainly names a file (no trailing slash, no empty, . or .. segment, though a
leading ./ is fine), and its name is short enough for the scratch name placed next to it.

Exit status. 0: placed. 1: refused, because the download failed or ran past its deadline, the bytes went over the cap
or hashed to something else, the run was interrupted, or the program could not clear the destination or its scratch
files. 2: malformed arguments. A status of 1 or 2 leaves nothing written, apart from any name the refusal reports as
not removed.
"""
import os  # already loaded while Python started, so nothing beside this file can stand in for it
import sys

# SIGKILL and SIGSTOP cannot be blocked, and the fault signals must not be: with one blocked, a genuine fault in this
# program never ends it on macOS. So a fault signal from another process acts as SIGKILL would, skipping the cleanup.
UNBLOCKABLE_NAMES = ("SIGKILL", "SIGSTOP", "SIGSEGV", "SIGBUS", "SIGFPE", "SIGILL", "SIGTRAP", "SIGSYS", "SIGABRT")
# Variables telling Python or the crypto library to load code from elsewhere. The restart and the download process
# never see them; proxy and certificate settings stay, since the pin decides what is kept whoever serves it.
STRIPPED_PREFIXES = ("PYTHON", "OPENSSL_", "DYLD_", "LD_")
# On macOS these choose the interpreter that sys.executable names, which the restart would run.
LAUNCHERS = ("PYTHONEXECUTABLE", "__PYVENV_LAUNCHER__")


def shown(text):
    """Render text as one printable ASCII line, writing backslashes, control characters and non-ASCII as escapes."""
    return "".join(c if " " <= c <= "~" and c != "\\" else c.encode("unicode_escape").decode("ascii")
                   for c in str(text))


def say(text, stream):
    """The one place the helper prints, so no message can skip the escaping."""
    print(shown(text), file=stream)


def leave(status):
    """Flush both streams here, where a failure can be ignored, and end the process with status, so Python's own flush
    at shutdown can neither change the status nor print a line of its own."""
    for stream in (sys.stdout, sys.stderr):
        try:
            stream.flush()
        except BaseException:
            pass
    os._exit(status)


def clean_environment(environ):
    """The environment the restart and the download process get: the given one without any variable that would make
    Python or OpenSSL load other code."""
    return {key: value for key, value in environ.items()
            if not key.startswith(STRIPPED_PREFIXES) and key not in LAUNCHERS}


if __name__ == "__main__":
    for launcher in LAUNCHERS:
        if launcher in os.environ:
            try:
                say(f"fetch-pinned: malformed: {launcher} is set, and it would choose the interpreter that runs this",
                    sys.stderr)
            except BaseException:
                pass
            leave(2)
    if (not (sys.flags.isolated and sys.flags.dont_write_bytecode and sys.flags.no_site)
            or clean_environment(os.environ) != dict(os.environ)):
        # Started any other way (through the interpreter, with Python or OpenSSL variables set), the program first
        # becomes itself again, isolated, without bytecode or site hooks and with a clean environment, before it
        # imports anything a folder, an installed package or the environment could supply.
        os.execve(sys.executable, [sys.executable, "-I", "-B", "-S", os.path.abspath(__file__), *sys.argv[1:]],
                  clean_environment(os.environ))

import signal  # noqa: E402  (after the re-exec on purpose)

if __name__ == "__main__":
    signal.pthread_sigmask(signal.SIG_BLOCK, signal.valid_signals() - {getattr(signal, name)
                                                                      for name in UNBLOCKABLE_NAMES
                                                                      if hasattr(signal, name)})
    signal.signal(signal.SIGCHLD, signal.SIG_DFL)  # a launcher may have left it ignored, which exec keeps

import hashlib  # noqa: E402
import http.client  # noqa: E402
import ipaddress  # noqa: E402
import math  # noqa: E402
import re  # noqa: E402
import secrets  # noqa: E402
import select  # noqa: E402
import socket  # noqa: E402
import stat  # noqa: E402
import subprocess  # noqa: E402
import threading  # noqa: E402
import time  # noqa: E402
import urllib.error  # noqa: E402
import urllib.parse  # noqa: E402
import urllib.request  # noqa: E402

MAX_BYTES = 512 * 1024 * 1024
TIMEOUT_SECONDS = 120
DEADLINE_SECONDS = 900
POLL_SECONDS = 0.05
MESSAGE_BYTES = 4096
KILL_WAIT_SECONDS = 10
CHUNK = 1024 * 1024
HEX64 = re.compile(r"[0-9A-Fa-f]{64}")
PRINTABLE = re.compile(r"[\x21-\x7e]+")
CONTROL = re.compile(r"[\x00-\x1f\x7f-\x9f]")
LABEL = re.compile(r"(?!-)[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,63}(?<!-)")

UNBLOCKED = frozenset(getattr(signal, name) for name in UNBLOCKABLE_NAMES if hasattr(signal, name))
# Blocked signals that by default do nothing, or only pause or continue the process; any other pending one is a stop
# request, the real-time signals included.
QUIET = frozenset(getattr(signal, name) for name in ("SIGCHLD", "SIGWINCH", "SIGURG", "SIGCONT", "SIGTSTP", "SIGTTIN",
                                                     "SIGTTOU", "SIGINFO") if hasattr(signal, name))
STOPS = frozenset(signal.valid_signals() - UNBLOCKED - QUIET)

# What the download process runs: unblock every signal it inherited blocked, compile this file's source text (never a
# cached or planted bytecode file), and fetch.
CHILD_CODE = """import signal, sys
signal.pthread_sigmask(signal.SIG_SETMASK, set())
with open(sys.argv[1], "rb") as source:
    code = compile(source.read(), sys.argv[1], "exec")
namespace = {"__name__": "fetch_pinned_download", "__file__": sys.argv[1]}
exec(code, namespace)
sys.exit(namespace["download_child"](sys.argv[2], int(sys.argv[3])))
"""
REPORTED = 3  # the download process's status when it printed its own reason


class Refused(Exception):
    """The file was not placed."""


class Malformed(Refused):
    """The arguments are wrong; nothing was fetched."""


class TooLarge(Refused):
    """The bytes passed the cap."""


class Leftover(Exception):
    """The file was placed, but a scratch name could not be removed; main reports it as a warning with status 0."""


class HttpsOnlyRedirect(urllib.request.HTTPRedirectHandler):
    def redirect_request(self, req, fp, code, msg, headers, newurl):
        try:
            check_url(newurl)  # the same rule as the first URL: https, a host, no user name or password
        except Malformed as error:
            raise Refused(f"a redirect target is not allowed: {error}") from None
        return super().redirect_request(req, fp, code, msg, headers, newurl)


def https_only_opener():
    return urllib.request.build_opener(HttpsOnlyRedirect)


def check_url(url):
    """Reject, before anything is downloaded, any URL that urllib could not send or resolve exactly as written: text
    outside printable ASCII (spaces, control characters, letters beyond ASCII), an opening bracket with no closing
    one, a port outside 1 to 65535 or not numeric, and a host that is neither an address nor a well-formed name."""
    if not isinstance(url, str) or not PRINTABLE.fullmatch(url):
        raise Malformed("the URL must be printable ASCII, with no space or control character")
    try:
        parts = urllib.parse.urlsplit(url)
        host, port = parts.hostname, parts.port
    except ValueError as error:
        raise Malformed(f"the URL cannot be read: {error}") from None
    if port == 0:
        raise Malformed("the URL's port must be 1 to 65535")
    if parts.scheme.lower() != "https" or not host:
        raise Malformed("the URL must be https with a host")
    if not valid_host(host):
        raise Malformed("the URL's host must be an address or a well-formed host name")
    if parts.username is not None or parts.password is not None:
        raise Malformed("the URL must not carry a user name or password")


def valid_host(host):
    """True for an address, or for a name made of dot-separated labels (each 1 to 63 letters, digits, underscores or
    hyphens not at either end, 253 characters at most overall, with one optional trailing dot). A host the system's
    own address parser reads as an address (hex, octal or short forms such as 0x7f000001, 017.0.0.1 or 127.1) must
    be written as one, and a name's last label is not all digits, since the resolver would read it as an address
    too. inet_aton parses locally and looks nothing up."""
    try:
        ipaddress.ip_address(host)
        return True
    except ValueError:
        pass
    labels = (host[:-1] if host.endswith(".") else host).split(".")
    try:
        socket.inet_aton(".".join(labels))
        return False
    except OSError:
        pass
    return (0 < len(".".join(labels)) <= 253 and all(LABEL.fullmatch(label) for label in labels)
            and not labels[-1].isdigit())


FOLDER = os.O_RDONLY | os.O_DIRECTORY | os.O_NOFOLLOW


def open_destination(dest, root):
    """Return (folder handle, file name) for dest. Each folder below root is opened in turn, refusing links, and all
    later work goes through the last handle; a link, or a folder replaced once the check is done, therefore cannot
    redirect the write outside root. The destination has to be missing or a regular file."""
    root = os.path.realpath(root)
    if not dest:
        raise Malformed("the destination must name a file")
    if CONTROL.search(dest):
        raise Malformed("the destination must not hold a control character")
    try:
        dest.encode("utf-8")
    except UnicodeEncodeError:
        raise Malformed("the destination must be valid UTF-8 text") from None
    pieces = (dest[2:] if dest.startswith("./") else dest).split("/")
    if any(piece in ("", ".", "..") for piece in (pieces[1:] if os.path.isabs(dest) else pieces)):
        raise Malformed("the destination must be a plain file path: no trailing slash, and no empty, . or .. segment")
    path = os.path.normpath(dest if os.path.isabs(dest) else os.path.join(root, dest))
    prefixes = [root]
    shell = os.path.normpath(os.environ.get("PWD") or ".")  # the shell's spelling of the same folder, if it is one
    if os.path.isabs(shell) and os.path.realpath(shell) == root:
        prefixes.append(shell)
    inside = [prefix for prefix in prefixes if path != prefix and os.path.commonpath([prefix, path]) == prefix]
    if not inside:  # whole segments compared: / as the folder holds any path; /a/work does not contain /a/workshop
        raise Malformed("the destination is outside the folder the helper runs in")
    parts = os.path.relpath(path, inside[0]).split(os.sep)
    if any(part in ("", ".", "..") for part in parts):
        raise Malformed("the destination must be a plain path to a file")
    try:
        folder = os.open(root, os.O_RDONLY | os.O_DIRECTORY)
    except OSError as error:
        raise Malformed(f"the folder the helper runs in cannot be opened: {error.strerror}") from None
    try:
        for part in parts[:-1]:
            try:
                inner = os.open(part, FOLDER, dir_fd=folder)
            except OSError:
                raise Malformed("every folder in the destination path must exist and be a real folder, not a link")
            close_quietly(folder)
            folder = inner
        try:
            longest = os.fpathconf(folder, "PC_NAME_MAX")
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            longest = 255
        if len(temporary_name(parts[-1]).encode()) > longest:  # its temporary name must fit beside it
            raise Malformed("the destination's name is too long for the temporary name the helper puts beside it")
        try:
            info = os.stat(parts[-1], dir_fd=folder, follow_symlinks=False)
        except FileNotFoundError:
            return folder, parts[-1]
        if not stat.S_ISREG(info.st_mode):
            raise Malformed("the destination must be missing or a regular file; a link, folder or device is refused")
        return folder, parts[-1]
    except BaseException:
        close_quietly(folder)
        raise


class Outcome:
    """The run's record of its one commit point: placed is set by a single store once the verified file is in place,
    and the cleanup and main read it. A record belongs to one run: one already marked placed is refused."""

    def __init__(self):
        self.placed = False


def pending_stop():
    """The reason to stop if a stop signal is waiting (only blocked signals can wait), or None. Reading it leaves the
    signal pending; the process ends with the run."""
    waiting = signal.sigpending() & STOPS
    if waiting:
        return "interrupted by " + ", ".join(sorted(signal_name(number) for number in waiting))
    return None


def signal_name(number):
    """A signal's name, or its number for one Python has no name for (a real-time signal on Linux)."""
    try:
        return signal.Signals(number).name
    except ValueError:
        return f"signal {number}"


def halt(end, stop):
    """Refuse the run if a stop was asked for or the deadline has passed. It is consulted at fixed points only, so a
    refusal from here is an ordinary exception and the cleanup that follows runs in full."""
    reason = stop() if stop is not None else None
    if reason:
        raise Refused(reason)
    if end is not None and time.monotonic() >= end:
        raise Refused("the fetch passed its deadline")


def close_quietly(handle):
    """Close a handle, ignoring errors; every close in the program goes through here. Closing cannot change the
    result: by then the data was flushed and checked, or the run has already failed, so a close error never swaps
    one failure for another and never turns a success into a failure."""
    try:
        os.close(handle)
    except OSError:
        pass


def remove(folder, name):
    """The one way the helper removes a name. A name already gone is fine; any other failure is a refusal that names
    what was left, so exit 1 never hides a leftover."""
    try:
        os.unlink(name, dir_fd=folder)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        pass
    except OSError as error:
        raise Refused(f"could not remove {name}: {error.strerror or error}") from None


def temporary_name(name):
    """The one shape of every temporary name: hidden, beside the destination, random, and marked as partial."""
    return f".{name}.{secrets.token_hex(8)}.part"


def identity(info):
    return info.st_dev, info.st_ino


def remove_own(folder, name, own):
    """Remove name only while it still holds the file this run made or read (own is its device and inode). A name
    whose file is not known yet (own is None: the run was cut off between creating it and recording it) is removed
    as it stands: in that instant it holds this run's new file unless another process raced it, which the threat
    model leaves out."""
    if own is not None:
        try:
            if identity(os.stat(name, dir_fd=folder, follow_symlinks=False)) != own:
                return
        except FileNotFoundError:
            return
    remove(folder, name)


def scratch(folder, name, pending, handles):
    """Create a fresh scratch file next to the destination and return (its name, an open read-write handle). The name
    is added to pending before the file is created and its identity noted right after; the handle goes into handles.
    That lets the cleanup delete and close both after a failure at any point."""
    temporary = temporary_name(name)
    entry = [temporary, None]
    pending.append(entry)
    handle = os.open(temporary, os.O_RDWR | os.O_CREAT | os.O_EXCL | os.O_NOFOLLOW, 0o600, dir_fd=folder)
    handles.append(handle)
    entry[1] = identity(os.fstat(handle))
    return temporary, handle


def copy_body(read, handle, max_bytes, end=None, stop=None):
    """Write everything read() returns into handle, stopping at the cap, and looking for a stop or the deadline before
    each chunk. The download process runs this on the network reply; the program runs it on a file it reuses."""
    size = 0
    while True:
        halt(end, stop)
        chunk = read(CHUNK)
        if not chunk:
            return size
        size += len(chunk)
        if size > max_bytes:
            raise TooLarge(f"the bytes pass the cap of {max_bytes}")
        view = memoryview(chunk)
        while view:
            view = view[os.write(handle, view):]


def finish(handle, max_bytes, mode=0o644):
    """Flush the scratch file, set its mode, then measure and hash it from its first byte through this handle, so the
    result describes exactly the bytes that will be renamed into place, whoever wrote them."""
    os.fsync(handle)
    os.fchmod(handle, mode)
    size = os.fstat(handle).st_size
    if size > max_bytes:
        raise TooLarge(f"the bytes pass the cap of {max_bytes}")
    hasher, offset = hashlib.sha256(), 0
    while offset < size:
        chunk = os.pread(handle, min(CHUNK, size - offset), offset)
        if not chunk:
            break
        hasher.update(chunk)
        offset += len(chunk)
    if offset != size:
        raise Refused("the scratch file changed while it was checked")
    return hasher.hexdigest()


def to_temporary(folder, name, read, max_bytes, pending, handles, mode=0o644, end=None, stop=None):
    """Copy what read() returns into a fresh scratch file; return (scratch name, SHA-256, open handle)."""
    temporary, handle = scratch(folder, name, pending, handles)
    copy_body(read, handle, max_bytes, end, stop)
    return temporary, finish(handle, max_bytes, mode), handle


def https_open(url):
    return https_only_opener().open(url, timeout=TIMEOUT_SECONDS)


def in_process(open_url):
    """A download that runs in this process: open_url(url) gives a reply, and its body is copied into the scratch
    handle under the cap. The download process runs exactly this with the https-only opener; the tests run it with a
    stand-in for the network."""
    def download(url, handle, max_bytes, end, stop):
        try:
            with open_url(url) as body:
                copy_body(body.read, handle, max_bytes, end, stop)
        except (OSError, http.client.HTTPException, urllib.error.URLError, ValueError) as error:
            raise Refused(f"the fetch failed: {error}") from None
    return download


def download_child(url, max_bytes):
    """The download process's whole job: check the URL again, fetch it into standard output under the cap, and on any
    failure print one escaped reason and return REPORTED. It never raises. It ends itself as soon as the program that
    started it is gone, so a program killed outright leaves no download running."""
    parent = os.getppid()

    def watch():
        while os.getppid() == parent:
            time.sleep(0.2)
        os._exit(REPORTED)

    threading.Thread(target=watch, daemon=True).start()
    try:
        check_url(url)
        in_process(https_open)(url, sys.stdout.fileno(), max_bytes, None, None)
        return 0
    except BaseException as error:
        try:
            reason = described(error) if isinstance(error, Refused) else f"the fetch failed: {type(error).__name__}"
            say(reason[:(MESSAGE_BYTES - 1) // 10], sys.stderr)  # an escape is at most 10 characters long
        except BaseException:
            pass
        return REPORTED


def child_argv(url, max_bytes, prefix=None):
    """The download process's command: this file run in Python's isolated mode, given the URL and the cap. prefix
    replaces everything before the URL; the tests use it to stand in for the network."""
    head = list(prefix) if prefix is not None else [sys.executable, "-I", "-B", "-S", "-c", CHILD_CODE,
                                                    os.path.abspath(__file__)]
    return head + [url, str(max_bytes)]


def spawned(prefix=None):
    """The program's download: start the download process in a process group of its own, with a cleaned environment
    and the scratch file as its standard output, and wait for it while looking for a stop request, the deadline and
    the cap. Its error pipe is emptied as it waits without ever blocking, keeping at most MESSAGE_BYTES, and is not
    read to its end. Its exit is seen without reaping it, so the group is killed while its id is still held, and
    only then is the process reaped, waiting at most KILL_WAIT_SECONDS."""
    def download(url, handle, max_bytes, end, stop):
        process = subprocess.Popen(child_argv(url, max_bytes, prefix), stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, stdout=handle,
                                   stderr=subprocess.PIPE, close_fds=True, start_new_session=True,
                                   env=clean_environment(os.environ))
        pipe = process.stderr.fileno()
        os.set_blocking(pipe, False)
        message, readable = bytearray(), [True]

        def drain():
            for _ in range(64):  # bounded, so writers cannot keep the program here
                try:
                    chunk = os.read(pipe, 65536)
                except BlockingIOError:
                    return
                except OSError:
                    readable[0] = False
                    return
                if not chunk:
                    readable[0] = False
                    return
                message.extend(chunk[:max(0, MESSAGE_BYTES - len(message))])

        lingering = False
        try:
            while True:
                drain()
                try:
                    if os.waitid(os.P_PID, process.pid, os.WEXITED | os.WNOHANG | os.WNOWAIT) is not None:
                        break  # exited, not yet reaped
                except ChildProcessError:
                    break
                halt(end, stop)
                if os.fstat(handle).st_size > max_bytes:
                    raise TooLarge(f"the bytes pass the cap of {max_bytes}")
                select.select([pipe] if readable[0] else [], [], [], POLL_SECONDS)
        finally:
            try:
                os.killpg(process.pid, signal.SIGKILL)  # its whole group, while the exited leader still holds the id
            except OSError:
                pass
            try:
                process.wait(timeout=KILL_WAIT_SECONDS)
            except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
                lingering = True
            drain()
            try:
                process.stderr.close()
            except Exception:
                pass
        if lingering:
            raise Refused("the download process did not end after SIGKILL")
        halt(None, stop)  # a stop that came as the download process ended outranks the reason it gave
        status = process.returncode
        if status == 0:
            return
        if status == REPORTED and bytes(message).strip():
            raise Refused(bytes(message).decode("utf-8", "replace").strip())
        if status < 0:
            raise Refused(f"the download process was ended by {signal_name(-status)}")
        raise Refused(f"the download process failed with status {status}")
    return download


def commit(state):
    """Make the placement final: the checked file is already at the destination, and a single assignment marks the run
    placed. The cleanup consults that mark: without it the placement is undone, with it the destination is kept."""
    state.placed = True


def same_file(info, other):
    return identity(info) == identity(other)


def discard(folder, pending):
    """Remove every pending temporary file now (a copy that did not verify), not at the end; an entry leaves the
    list only once its removal succeeded, so a failure here is retried and named by the final cleanup."""
    while pending:
        temporary, own = pending[0]
        remove_own(folder, temporary, own)
        del pending[0]


def clear_destination(folder, name, pending, cleared):
    """After a refusal, remove from the destination a file this run renamed there (one whose identity a pending
    entry holds), only while the name still holds it, so exit 1 never leaves a file written. If the refusal came
    before the destination was cleared (set aside, or removed where it stood), whatever file is there was never
    verified, so it is removed as it stands; a folder is never removed. It is called only for a run that was not
    placed."""
    owns = [own for _, own in pending if own is not None]
    if cleared and not owns:
        return
    try:
        info = os.stat(name, dir_fd=folder, follow_symlinks=False)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return
    if stat.S_ISDIR(info.st_mode):
        return
    if not cleared or identity(info) in owns:
        remove(folder, name)


def settle(folder, temporary, handle, found, digest, name):
    """Rename the temporary file onto the destination only when its digest matches, and only when the name still
    holds the file this helper wrote and hashed, before and after the rename."""
    if found != digest:
        return False
    written = os.fstat(handle)
    if not same_file(os.stat(temporary, dir_fd=folder, follow_symlinks=False), written):
        raise Refused("the temporary file was replaced before it was placed")
    os.rename(temporary, name, src_dir_fd=folder, dst_dir_fd=folder)
    if not same_file(os.stat(name, dir_fd=folder, follow_symlinks=False), written):
        remove(folder, name)
        raise Refused("the destination was replaced while it was placed")
    return True


def set_aside(folder, name, pending):
    """Move whatever occupies the destination to a scratch name with a single atomic rename, before reading any of it.
    This is the program's first change there; afterward the name is vacant or holds a checked file at all times,
    process death included. If the rename fails, the file is removed where it stands, or the refusal lists it; a
    failure earlier in the run has the cleanup delete it in the same way. Returns the pending entry, or None if
    nothing was there. A folder is moved back and the run refused, because it cannot contain downloaded bytes and
    deleting a directory tree is out of scope; a link or device is refused and left for the cleanup to delete."""
    entry = [temporary_name(name), None]
    pending.append(entry)
    try:
        os.rename(name, entry[0], src_dir_fd=folder, dst_dir_fd=folder)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        pending.remove(entry)
        return None
    except OSError:  # it cannot be set aside, so it is removed where it stands, or the refusal names it
        pending.remove(entry)
        remove(folder, name)
        return None
    info = os.stat(entry[0], dir_fd=folder, follow_symlinks=False)
    entry[1] = identity(info)
    if stat.S_ISDIR(info.st_mode):
        os.rename(entry[0], name, src_dir_fd=folder, dst_dir_fd=folder)
        pending.remove(entry)
        raise Refused("the destination changed during the run into a folder")
    if not stat.S_ISREG(info.st_mode):
        raise Refused("the destination changed during the run into something other than a regular file")
    return entry


def place(url, digest, folder, name, download, max_bytes, end=None, stop=None, state=None):
    """All file work here uses the folder handle; the path is not consulted again. An existing destination is moved
    away first, then copied to a scratch file and hashed as that copy, so a good result never depends on reading a
    name twice. A single cleanup is responsible for every scratch file and for anything this run put at the
    destination, deleting each only while its name still refers to that file. It reads the placed mark, so once a
    run is marked placed, no later error removes the destination."""
    state = state if state is not None else Outcome()
    pending, handles, failure, cleared = [], [], None, False
    try:
        halt(end, stop)  # the last moment at which the destination is still untouched
        aside = set_aside(folder, name, pending)
        cleared = True  # the name is empty now, or its file is set aside under a temporary name
        if aside is not None:
            found = None
            try:
                handle = os.open(aside[0], os.O_RDONLY | os.O_NOFOLLOW | os.O_NONBLOCK, dir_fd=folder)
            except OSError:
                handle = None  # it cannot be read, so it cannot be verified: it goes like any other wrong file
            if handle is not None:
                try:
                    found_info = os.fstat(handle)
                    if identity(found_info) != aside[1] or not stat.S_ISREG(found_info.st_mode):
                        raise Refused("the destination changed during the run")
                    mode = stat.S_IMODE(found_info.st_mode)  # the copy keeps the existing file's mode bits
                    try:
                        temporary, found, copy = to_temporary(folder, name, lambda size: os.read(handle, size),
                                                              max_bytes, pending, handles, mode, end, stop)
                    except TooLarge:
                        found = None  # over the cap, so it is not the pinned file
                finally:
                    close_quietly(handle)
            if found == digest:
                remove_own(folder, aside[0], aside[1])  # the verified copy replaces it, so the original goes first
                pending.remove(aside)
                if settle(folder, temporary, copy, found, digest, name):
                    halt(end, stop)  # the last check before the commit point
                    commit(state)
                    return
            discard(folder, pending)  # the file set aside and its copy did not verify: both go before the fetch
        _, received = scratch(folder, name, pending, handles)
        download(url, received, max_bytes, end, stop)
        offset = [0]

        def read_received(size):
            chunk = os.pread(received, size, offset[0])
            offset[0] += len(chunk)
            return chunk

        # The download process's file may still be open somewhere; the bytes are copied into one only this process
        # ever had open, hashed there, and that copy is what goes into place.
        temporary, found, written = to_temporary(folder, name, read_received, max_bytes, pending, handles, 0o644, end,
                                                 stop)
        if not settle(folder, temporary, written, found, digest, name):
            raise Refused(f"digest mismatch: expected {digest}, got {found}")
        halt(end, stop)  # the last check before the commit point
        commit(state)
    except BaseException as error:
        failure = error
        raise
    finally:
        # Each step below is attempted regardless of how the previous ones went.
        for handle in handles:
            try:
                close_quietly(handle)
            except Exception:
                pass
        left = []
        for temporary, own in pending:  # only leftovers this run made, each while it still holds that file
            try:
                remove_own(folder, temporary, own)
            except Exception:
                left.append(temporary)
        if not state.placed:  # undo an unfinished placement, or remove a file the run never got to move away
            try:
                clear_destination(folder, name, pending, cleared)
            except Exception:
                left.append(name)
        # No leftover goes unnamed: before the commit point the refusal names it, after the reason the run failed, if
        # any; after it, the placement stands and main prints the name as a warning.
        if left and not state.placed:
            reason = f" after: {failure}" if failure is not None else ""
            raise Refused(f"could not remove {', '.join(left)}{reason}") from failure
        if left:
            raise Leftover(f"could not remove {', '.join(left)}")


def fetch_pinned(url, digest, dest, root=None, download=None, max_bytes=MAX_BYTES, deadline_seconds=DEADLINE_SECONDS,
                 stop=None, outcome=None):
    """Put url's body at dest if its SHA-256 equals digest, and raise Refused if not. An existing file at dest is
    reused without a download only when a fresh copy of it hashes correctly; anything else there is cleared first,
    so after argument checking, dest holds only checked content whether the call returns or raises. download fetches
    into the scratch handle (by default the download process); stop reports a waiting stop request (by default a
    pending stop signal). The deadline starts once the arguments pass. Malformed: a deadline other than a positive
    finite number, a byte cap that is negative or not an integer, and an outcome record from an earlier run."""
    check_url(url)
    if not HEX64.fullmatch(digest or ""):
        raise Malformed("the digest must be 64 hex digits")
    if outcome is not None and outcome.placed:
        raise Malformed("the outcome record is from an earlier run; each run needs a fresh one")
    if (isinstance(deadline_seconds, bool) or not isinstance(deadline_seconds, (int, float))
            or not math.isfinite(deadline_seconds) or deadline_seconds <= 0):
        raise Malformed("the deadline must be a positive, finite number of seconds")
    if isinstance(max_bytes, bool) or not isinstance(max_bytes, int) or max_bytes < 0:
        raise Malformed("the byte cap must be a whole number, 0 or more")
    state = outcome if outcome is not None else Outcome()
    end = time.monotonic() + deadline_seconds
    folder = None
    try:
        folder, name = open_destination(dest, root if root is not None else os.getcwd())
        place(url, digest.lower(), folder, name, download if download is not None else spawned(), max_bytes, end,
              stop if stop is not None else pending_stop, state)
    finally:
        if folder is not None:
            close_quietly(folder)
    return dest


def described(error):
    """An exception's text, or its type's name when even making the text fails, so a report never raises."""
    try:
        return str(error)
    except Exception:
        return type(error).__name__


def last_resort(kind, value, trace):
    """Handle an exception that gets past main, such as one raised while a report is printed. Python's default output
    would include chained text without escaping, so this prints a single escaped line naming only the exception type.
    It never raises, because if it did Python would fall back to its full default output. The status is nonzero."""
    try:
        say(f"fetch-pinned: refused: {getattr(kind, '__name__', 'error')}", sys.stderr)
    except BaseException:
        pass


def refusal(error):
    """(exit status, the one line) for an exception that ended a run before its commit point."""
    if isinstance(error, Malformed):
        return 2, f"fetch-pinned: malformed: {described(error)}"
    if isinstance(error, Refused):
        return 1, f"fetch-pinned: refused: {described(error)}"
    if isinstance(error, OSError):  # any operating-system failure is a refusal; nothing unverified is left in place
        return 1, f"fetch-pinned: refused: {error.strerror or described(error)}"
    return 1, f"fetch-pinned: refused: {type(error).__name__}: {described(error)}"  # an interrupt included


def main(argv, child=None, deadline_seconds=DEADLINE_SECONDS):
    """Run once and return the exit status; a report that cannot be printed never changes it, and a failure after
    placement keeps exit 0 and is printed as one warning line. child replaces the download process's command up to
    the URL (see child_argv); the tests use it."""
    if len(argv) != 4:
        try:
            say("fetch-pinned: usage: fetch-pinned <https-url> <sha256> <destination>", sys.stderr)
        except BaseException:
            pass
        return 2
    outcome = Outcome()
    late = None
    try:
        fetch_pinned(argv[1], argv[2], argv[3], download=spawned(child), deadline_seconds=deadline_seconds,
                     outcome=outcome)
    except BaseException as error:
        late = error
        if not outcome.placed:  # before the commit point: rolled back, so the run is refused
            status = 1
            try:
                status, line = refusal(error)
                say(line, sys.stderr)
            except BaseException:
                pass
            return status
    try:  # past the commit point the file is verified and in place, so even a report that fails is exit 0
        say(f"fetch-pinned: placed {argv[3]}", sys.stdout)
    except BaseException:
        pass
    if late is not None:  # after placement: reported, not swallowed
        try:
            say(f"fetch-pinned: warning: {type(late).__name__}: {described(late)}", sys.stderr)
        except BaseException:
            pass
    return 0


def command(argv, child=None, deadline_seconds=DEADLINE_SECONDS):
    """The executable's entry. Its first act blocks every signal that can be blocked, so that from here to exit no
    handler runs, and puts SIGCHLD back to its default; then it sets the last-resort report and runs."""
    signal.pthread_sigmask(signal.SIG_BLOCK, signal.valid_signals() - UNBLOCKED)  # already so when run as a file
    signal.signal(signal.SIGCHLD, signal.SIG_DFL)
    sys.excepthook = last_resort
    return main(argv, child, deadline_seconds)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    leave(command(sys.argv))
